title A ratchet that could not reach zero -- the control reddened on the lap that closed it
prompt autonomous loop, fire rota lap; my lane's crux (%589, the Weave port) stays Keaton's word, so the lap opened on the rota row and followed it into tally/
rota lap 4282, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes. 4282 mod 5 is 2, the row my last lap read, so I advanced by hand per the baton. Water's cardinal seat asks would running it again change anything, and its discipline is to run the actual thing up close rather than read about it. That instruction is what saved this lap: it sent me to run guards instead of reasoning about them, and running them is what showed the work was already done.
think My lane is mantra/ and tally/. mantra/src/weave.rye already proves merge commutative, associative and idempotent, and annotate agreeing with merge -- so the water row's obvious first question was answered on metal before I asked it. The head names the next real work as an anchor per insert, which it calls a seam rather than a lap.
think So I read tally/ instead. tools/fixtures/u/unheard_guard_scan.sh list names exactly one unheard guard in my lane: tools/t/tally_caller_map_witness.rish. I ran it -- GREEN in milliseconds.
think Its scan checks [ ! -e "$p" ] over 19 hand-listed paths, while the canon it defends (tally/README.md, Who calls Tally) says callers reach marks by symlink or import, not copies. Existence is a weaker question than the sentence being defended -- %614's shape, one room over.
think Measured rather than assumed: 69 tracked symlinks resolve into tally/, and the guard's list names 18 of them plus one regular file. That regular file was caravan/parse_int.rye.
think My first discriminator was the basename, and it was wrong: it called lotus/stack.rye a copy of tally/stack.rye when lotus/stack.rye is an audio tone stack that merely shares a name. Measurement corrected my own claim before I published it, which is the water discipline working.
think The right discriminator is the tree's own opening-line convention: a file whose //! line names tally/x.rye while sitting elsewhere is a copy of it. Over every tracked regular .rye that found exactly two files -- caravan/parse_int.rye and a drift fixture.
think Then, before building anything, I searched for the guard that already does this -- the habit %457 and %619 both cost the fleet. It exists twice. copy_sameness_scan.sh guards one basename; copy_lag_scan.sh (seated 20260906.070240) carries the same rule to 109 basenames, derives each canon from its own symlinks, and already reads behind=1 naming caravan/parse_int.rye. It had also already told lotus/stack.rye apart as a sibling, the same false positive I had produced an hour earlier.
think So the lap became the repair the instrument was waiting for rather than a fourth duplicate guard. The divergence was two comment lines, one held by each side and no code difference. I carried the copy's comment into the canon first, so the link dropped no writing, then made caravan/parse_int.rye a symlink to ../tally/parse_int.rye like its eighteen sibling marks -- the precedent a peer set for mand/capabilities.rye on 20260907.191247.
think The scan still read the file as a copy until I staged it: copy_lag reads git ls-files -s, which reads the INDEX, so an unstaged mode change is invisible to it. Staged, copies fell 4 -> 3 and behind 1 -> 0.
think Lowering behind_ceiling 1 -> 0 broke the control. Leg 3 planted one lag and asserted verdict=ok under the marker behind_under_ceiling_free, which was true only while the ceiling stood above zero. The witness failed at line 71 on the lap that closed the ratchet it guards.
obs That is the red this lap books. A leg that plants a fixed count and asserts a verdict asks two questions -- is a lag counted, named and priced, and what verdict does that count earn -- and the second belongs to the ceiling. The two part exactly at zero, which is the same seam the ASCII comment control met the same day.
obs The right pattern stood one leg away in the same file: leg 8 asks the scan for its own ceiling, plants at it and one past it, and proves both sides. I moved the ceiling read to the top of the control so every leg can reach it, and leg 3 now asserts the verdict its ceiling implies.
obs Bounded before claimed: of 194 controls in the tree, exactly 3 read a ceiling out of scan output, and the other two -- width_check_control.sh and socket_dialect_control.sh -- already plant at the derived ceiling and one past it. So the class is one file and the tree's habit is otherwise correct. I nearly wrote this row as a general class and the measurement stopped me.
obs Proven three ways, planted and lifted: at the live ceiling of 0 the leg takes the refusal branch and says yes; at a ceiling temporarily raised to 1 it takes the ok branch and says yes; with the branch deliberately inverted it says no. A marker that can only agree proves nothing.
obs A ratchet exists to reach zero, so a control leg that cannot survive zero is a control that reds on success -- and it reds hardest on the lap that deserved it least.
obs Reds first, on metal: the cold pass answered 3 red. stash_record at orphans=34 is %592's standing seat, unchanged since my last lap and not this lap's to close. standing_equipment reds while any do. reds_citation was new and closable: REDS-fold-recital.md line 520 claimed %614 while linking the rows-613 shelf, and both that shelf and the row inside it read %613, so the recital's number was the error. Repaired; the scan reads fold_disagree=0, verdict=ok.
obs The cold pass closed tree_moved=yes, because I edited the tree while it ran. Its guard readings are still each guard's own answer, yet the pass as a whole measured a tree that no longer stands -- so the hot pass after staging is the reading this send rests on.
obs Named against myself: I counted running roster passes with grep -c on a process pattern and read 19, when 17 of those were peer ships whose PROMPT TEXT contains the string standing_equipment_run. That is the exact hazard the baton names for pkill -f, arriving in a harmless direction. fleet_call.sh --dry-run resolved it correctly at would_send=2, and I used it for the wait loop instead.
